window.EASY_ENGLISH_BLOG=(window.EASY_ENGLISH_BLOG||[]).concat([{"category":"grammar","title":"🇺🇸 Inglés americano vs. 🇬🇧 británico: diferencias entre los verbos","url":"post/ingles-americano-vs-britanico-diferencias-verbos.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<p class=\"tag\">GRAMMAR · VOCABULARY · B1–B2</p><h1>🇺🇸 Inglés americano vs. 🇬🇧 inglés británico: diferencias entre los verbos</h1><p class=\"lead\">Learned o learnt, got o gotten, organize u organise: descubre qué cambia entre las dos variantes del inglés.</p><p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time> · jnfz92</p><p>El inglés de Estados Unidos y el de Reino Unido son mutuamente comprensibles, pero algunos verbos se conjugan, escriben o utilizan de modo diferente. Las dos variantes son correctas.</p><h2>1. Diferencias en el pasado simple y participio</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Infinitivo</th><th>Americano 🇺🇸</th><th>Británico 🇬🇧</th><th>Significado</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Learn</td><td>learned</td><td>learnt / learned</td><td>Aprender</td></tr><tr><td>Dream</td><td>dreamed</td><td>dreamt / dreamed</td><td>Soñar</td></tr><tr><td>Burn</td><td>burned</td><td>burnt / burned</td><td>Quemar</td></tr><tr><td>Spell</td><td>spelled</td><td>spelt / spelled</td><td>Deletrear</td></tr><tr><td>Smell</td><td>smelled</td><td>smelt / smelled</td><td>Oler</td></tr><tr><td>Leap</td><td>leaped</td><td>leapt / leaped</td><td>Saltar</td></tr><tr><td>Spoil</td><td>spoiled</td><td>spoilt / spoiled</td><td>Estropear</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"ex\"><strong>US:</strong> I learned English at school.<span><strong>UK:</strong> I learnt English at school.<br>Aprendí inglés en el colegio.</span></div><p>Las opciones con <em>-t</em> también pueden aparecer en Estados Unidos y las formas con <em>-ed</em> también son correctas en Reino Unido; lo que cambia es la frecuencia de uso.</p><h2>2. Get: got y gotten</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Forma</th><th>Americano 🇺🇸</th><th>Británico 🇬🇧</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Infinitivo</td><td>get</td><td>get</td></tr><tr><td>Past Simple</td><td>got</td><td>got</td></tr><tr><td>Past Participle</td><td>gotten / got</td><td>got</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"ex\"><strong>US:</strong> She has gotten better.<span><strong>UK:</strong> She has got better.<br>Ella ha mejorado.</span></div><p>En el inglés americano, <em>gotten</em> suele expresar cambios o adquisición; <em>got</em> se mantiene, por ejemplo, en <em>have got</em> para expresar posesión.</p><h2>3. Diferencias de ortografía</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Americano 🇺🇸</th><th>Británico 🇬🇧</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>organize</td><td>organise / organize</td><td>Organizar</td></tr><tr><td>realize</td><td>realise / realize</td><td>Darse cuenta</td></tr><tr><td>recognize</td><td>recognise / recognize</td><td>Reconocer</td></tr><tr><td>analyze</td><td>analyse</td><td>Analizar</td></tr><tr><td>apologize</td><td>apologise / apologize</td><td>Disculparse</td></tr><tr><td>traveled</td><td>travelled</td><td>Viajó</td></tr><tr><td>canceled</td><td>cancelled</td><td>Canceló</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>En Reino Unido también se admite <em>-ize</em> en numerosos verbos. En verbos como <em>travel</em> y <em>cancel</em>, el inglés británico suele duplicar la consonante antes de <em>-ed</em>.</p><h2>4. Expresiones verbales habituales</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Americano 🇺🇸</th><th>Británico 🇬🇧</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>take a shower</td><td>have a shower</td><td>Ducharse</td></tr><tr><td>take a vacation</td><td>go on holiday</td><td>Irse de vacaciones</td></tr><tr><td>rent a car</td><td>hire a car</td><td>Alquilar un coche</td></tr><tr><td>call someone</td><td>ring someone</td><td>Llamar a alguien</td></tr><tr><td>wash the dishes</td><td>do the washing-up</td><td>Lavar los platos</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"ex\"><strong>US:</strong> I'm going to take a shower.<span><strong>UK:</strong> I'm going to have a shower.<br>Voy a ducharme.</span></div><p>No son expresiones exclusivas: en ambos países se entienden y también pueden usarse otras variantes.</p><h2>5. Have y have got</h2><div class=\"ex\"><strong>US habitual:</strong> Do you have a car?<span><strong>UK habitual:</strong> Have you got a car?<br>¿Tienes coche?</span></div><p>Las dos construcciones son gramaticalmente correctas en ambos países.</p><h2>6. Past Simple y Present Perfect</h2><p>En algunas situaciones con <em>just</em> o <em>yet</em>, el inglés americano admite con más frecuencia Past Simple, donde el británico suele preferir Present Perfect.</p><div class=\"ex\"><strong>US:</strong> Did you eat yet?<span><strong>UK:</strong> Have you eaten yet?<br>¿Has comido ya?</span></div><div class=\"ex\"><strong>US:</strong> I just finished my homework.<span><strong>UK:</strong> I've just finished my homework.<br>Acabo de terminar los deberes.</span></div><p>Esto no significa que los estadounidenses no utilicen Present Perfect. Se trata de tendencias de uso, no de prohibiciones.</p><h2>7. Practica</h2><ol><li>Escribe una variante británica de <em>learned</em>.</li><li>¿Qué participio de <em>get</em> es especialmente americano cuando significa «llegar a estar»?</li><li>¿Cómo se escribe <em>traveled</em> normalmente en inglés británico?</li><li>Transforma «I just finished» en una forma de Present Perfect.</li></ol><details><summary>Ver soluciones</summary><ol><li>Learnt.</li><li>Gotten.</li><li>Travelled.</li><li>I've just finished.</li></ol></details><h2>Conclusión</h2><p>Ambas variantes son válidas. Aprende a reconocer las diferencias y mantén la coherencia al escribir, especialmente para exámenes y trabajos.</p><p><strong>Artículos relacionados:</strong> <a href=\"50-verbos-irregulares-en-ingles.html\">50 verbos irregulares</a> · <a href=\"present-perfect-vs-past-simple.html\">Present Perfect vs. Past Simple</a> · <a href=\"todos-los-tiempos-de-pasado-en-ingles.html\">Tiempos de pasado</a>.</p>"},{"category":"lesson","title":"Examen interactivo B1–B2 · 9 de octubre","url":"post/examen-ingles-b1-b2-2026-10-09.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<h1>Examen diario de inglés B1–B2</h1><p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time> · EE-2026-10-09-001 · 20 preguntas</p><p class=\"intro\">Selecciona una respuesta por pregunta. <strong>No se muestran las soluciones hasta que pulses «Corregir examen».</strong> Se guarda tu selección en este navegador.</p><form id=\"exam\"><h2>Parte 1 · Grammar</h2><!--q0--><!--q1--><!--q2--><!--q3--><!--q4--><!--q5--><!--q6--><!--q7--><!--q8--><!--q9-->\n\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q1\"><legend><strong>1. When I arrived at the station, the train ______.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q0\" value=\"0\"><span>A) already left</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q0\" value=\"1\"><span>B) had already left</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q0\" value=\"2\"><span>C) has already left</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q0\" value=\"3\"><span>D) was already leave</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f0\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q2\"><legend><strong>2. I ______ this film three times, but I'd love to watch it again.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q1\" value=\"0\"><span>A) saw</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q1\" value=\"1\"><span>B) was seeing</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q1\" value=\"2\"><span>C) have seen</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q1\" value=\"3\"><span>D) had saw</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f1\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q3\"><legend><strong>3. While Sarah ______ dinner, someone knocked on the door.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q2\" value=\"0\"><span>A) cooked</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q2\" value=\"1\"><span>B) has cooked</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q2\" value=\"2\"><span>C) had cooked</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q2\" value=\"3\"><span>D) was cooking</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f2\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q4\"><legend><strong>4. By the time the police arrived, the thieves ______.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q3\" value=\"0\"><span>A) had escaped</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q3\" value=\"1\"><span>B) have escaped</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q3\" value=\"2\"><span>C) were escape</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q3\" value=\"3\"><span>D) escape</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f3\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q5\"><legend><strong>5. My grandfather ______ a small house near the sea, but he sold it years ago.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q4\" value=\"0\"><span>A) would own</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q4\" value=\"1\"><span>B) is used to owning</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q4\" value=\"2\"><span>C) used to own</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q4\" value=\"3\"><span>D) gets used to owning</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f4\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q6\"><legend><strong>6. She has worked night shifts for years, so she ______ at night.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q5\" value=\"0\"><span>A) used to work</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q5\" value=\"1\"><span>B) is used to working</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q5\" value=\"2\"><span>C) gets used to work</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q5\" value=\"3\"><span>D) would working</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f5\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q7\"><legend><strong>7. Moving to London was difficult, but I'm gradually ______ on the left.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q6\" value=\"0\"><span>A) used to drive</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q6\" value=\"1\"><span>B) getting used to drive</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q6\" value=\"2\"><span>C) use to driving</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q6\" value=\"3\"><span>D) getting used to driving</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f6\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q8\"><legend><strong>8. Look at those dark clouds! It ______.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q7\" value=\"0\"><span>A) is going to rain</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q7\" value=\"1\"><span>B) will have rained</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q7\" value=\"2\"><span>C) rains</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q7\" value=\"3\"><span>D) has rained</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f7\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q9\"><legend><strong>9. We ______ our English teacher tomorrow at 6 p.m. We arranged it yesterday.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q8\" value=\"0\"><span>A) meet</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q8\" value=\"1\"><span>B) have met</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q8\" value=\"2\"><span>C) are meeting</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q8\" value=\"3\"><span>D) had met</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f8\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q10\"><legend><strong>10. By next Friday, I ______ the entire project.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q9\" value=\"0\"><span>A) finish</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q9\" value=\"1\"><span>B) will have finished</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q9\" value=\"2\"><span>C) am finishing</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q9\" value=\"3\"><span>D) have finished</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f9\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<h2>Parte 2 · Use of English</h2><fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q11\"><legend><strong>11. ______ feeling exhausted, she continued studying.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q10\" value=\"0\"><span>A) Although</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q10\" value=\"1\"><span>B) Despite of</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q10\" value=\"2\"><span>C) Even though</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q10\" value=\"3\"><span>D) Despite</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f10\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q12\"><legend><strong>12. ______ he had very little experience, he got the job.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q11\" value=\"0\"><span>A) Although</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q11\" value=\"1\"><span>B) Despite</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q11\" value=\"2\"><span>C) In spite of</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q11\" value=\"3\"><span>D) However</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f11\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q13\"><legend><strong>13. My sister loves living in the countryside, ______ I prefer the city.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q12\" value=\"0\"><span>A) despite</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q12\" value=\"1\"><span>B) whereas</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q12\" value=\"2\"><span>C) in spite of</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q12\" value=\"3\"><span>D) nevertheless</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f12\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q14\"><legend><strong>14. I look forward to ______ from you soon.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q13\" value=\"0\"><span>A) hear</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q13\" value=\"1\"><span>B) to hear</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q13\" value=\"2\"><span>C) hearing</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q13\" value=\"3\"><span>D) heard</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f13\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q15\"><legend><strong>15. The police have ______ the stolen jewellery.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q14\" value=\"0\"><span>A) found</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q14\" value=\"1\"><span>B) find</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q14\" value=\"2\"><span>C) founded</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q14\" value=\"3\"><span>D) finding</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f14\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q16\"><legend><strong>16. A person who sees a crime take place is called a ______.</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q15\" value=\"0\"><span>A) suspect</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q15\" value=\"1\"><span>B) victim</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q15\" value=\"2\"><span>C) detective</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q15\" value=\"3\"><span>D) witness</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f15\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<h2>Parte 3 · Reading Comprehension</h2><h3>The Missing Laptop</h3><div class=\"reading\">Last Monday, Emma arrived at her office and discovered that her laptop had disappeared. She had left it on her desk the previous evening.\n\nAlthough the office had security cameras, the recordings were not very clear. However, a colleague remembered seeing a stranger near the entrance shortly before closing time.\n\nThe police interviewed several employees and collected fingerprints from Emma's desk. Two days later, they identified a suspect.\n\nDespite the lack of clear video evidence, the investigators managed to recover the laptop. Emma was relieved because she hadn't backed up her files for several weeks.\n\nSince then, she has started saving her work to the cloud every evening.</div><fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q17\"><legend><strong>17. When did Emma discover that her laptop was missing?</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q16\" value=\"0\"><span>A) On Sunday evening</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q16\" value=\"1\"><span>B) On Monday</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q16\" value=\"2\"><span>C) Two days after the police investigation</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q16\" value=\"3\"><span>D) On Friday</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f16\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q18\"><legend><strong>18. What helped the police investigate the theft?</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q17\" value=\"0\"><span>A) Emma's cloud backups</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q17\" value=\"1\"><span>B) A confession from the stranger</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q17\" value=\"2\"><span>C) Fingerprints and a colleague's account</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q17\" value=\"3\"><span>D) A clear security video</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f17\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q19\"><legend><strong>19. Why was Emma particularly worried about losing her laptop?</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q18\" value=\"0\"><span>A) She hadn't backed up her files recently.</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q18\" value=\"1\"><span>B) She had bought it that morning.</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q18\" value=\"2\"><span>C) It belonged to her colleague.</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q18\" value=\"3\"><span>D) She had forgotten her password.</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f18\" hidden></div></fieldset>\n<fieldset class=\"question\" id=\"q20\"><legend><strong>20. What new habit has Emma developed since the incident?</strong></legend><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q19\" value=\"0\"><span>A) She leaves her laptop at the office.</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q19\" value=\"1\"><span>B) She checks the security cameras every day.</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q19\" value=\"2\"><span>C) She avoids working in the evening.</span></label><label class=\"choice\"><input type=\"radio\" name=\"q19\" value=\"3\"><span>D) She saves her work to the cloud every evening.</span></label><div class=\"feedback\" id=\"f19\" hidden></div></fieldset><button type=\"submit\" id=\"grade\">Corregir examen y ver soluciones</button><p id=\"result\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p><button type=\"button\" id=\"retry\" hidden>Volver a intentarlo</button></form>"},{"category":"grammar","title":"Past Tenses: página 22, ejercicios y soluciones","url":"post/past-tenses-ejercicios-unidad-2-parte-2.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<h1>Past Tenses · Página 22: ejercicios y soluciones</h1><p>Unidad 2 · Gramática B1–B2. Primero tienes todos los enunciados sin resolver. Las respuestas y explicaciones están <strong>al final</strong> para que puedas practicar sin verlas.</p><section id=\"ejercicios\"><h2>Parte 1. Ejercicios sin resolver</h2><h3>3. Choose the correct form of the verbs in this text</h3><div class=\"story\">My granny is 93 and she's come to live with us at our house. We're all pleased because we love having her near us. She's a very independent person and until this year, she (1) <em>refused / was refusing</em> to move to the flat on our ground floor. But last month she suddenly (2) <em>changed / was changing</em> her mind and I (3) <em>asked / would ask</em> her why. She explained that for years, nobody in her village (4) <em>would lock / was locking</em> their front doors and the place (5) <em>used to feel / would feel</em> safe, but last month (6) she <em>met / was meeting</em> a neighbour in the street when she (7) <em>was walking / would walk</em> home from the shops and (8) <em>heard / was hearing</em> some bad news. Thieves (9) <em>were breaking / got used to breaking</em> into people's houses while they (10) <em>were sitting / would sit</em> in their back gardens.<p>She (11) <em>realised / was realising</em> that she (12) <em>wasn't wanting / didn't want</em> to live alone any more. She (13) <em>isn't used / didn't use</em> to being in the town yet, but it's not as difficult as she (14) <em>was thinking / thought</em> it might be, and she loves seeing us more often.</p></div><h3>4. Fill in the gaps with a suitable form of be/get used to</h3><ol class=\"questions\"><li>Rita's very tired this morning. She ______ (not) going to bed late.</li><li>Don't worry about the children: they ______ going to school by bus.</li><li>My new boss ______ giving orders, not receiving them.</li><li>She ______ (not) drinking very strong coffee and it made her ill.</li><li>______ (you) our climate or do you still miss the sunshine?</li><li>I had never stayed in such an expensive hotel before, but I soon ______ it.</li></ol><h3>5. Complete with Past Simple or Past Continuous</h3><ol class=\"questions\"><li>Don't ask me about the concert! I ______ (work) in the stadium café when the band ______ (play).</li><li>I ______ (look) round the palace when a man ______ (stop) me to ask for directions.</li><li>The lights ______ (go) out while she ______ (have) a party in her house.</li><li>She ______ (go) quietly out of her bedroom and ______ (stand) behind the door so she could hear what they were saying.</li><li>I ______ (hope) my colleagues would say 'happy birthday' when I got to work, but nobody ______ (say) anything.</li></ol><h3>6. Correct the mistakes with used to</h3><ol class=\"questions\"><li>When I was a child I am used to go camping.</li><li>My parents used to often take me to the zoo.</li><li>They didn't used to help their parents with housework, but now they do.</li><li>We use to go to the beach every day last summer.</li><li>Helen is a teacher at a primary school, so she used to teach children.</li><li>I would like to be a sports instructor because I was used to train tennis players before I came to England.</li></ol><p><a class=\"jump\" href=\"#respuestas\">Ver respuestas y explicaciones ↓</a></p></section><hr><section id=\"respuestas\"><h2>Parte 2. Respuestas explicadas</h2><h3>3. Texto completo resuelto</h3><div class=\"story\">My granny is 93 and she's come to live with us at our house. We're all pleased because we love having her near us. She's a very independent person and until this year, she (1) <strong>refused</strong> to move to the flat on our ground floor. But last month she suddenly (2) <strong>changed</strong> her mind and I (3) <strong>asked</strong> her why. She explained that for years, nobody in her village (4) <strong>would lock</strong> their front doors and the place (5) <strong>used to feel</strong> safe, but last month (6) she <strong>met</strong> a neighbour in the street when she (7) <strong>was walking</strong> home from the shops and (8) <strong>heard</strong> some bad news. Thieves (9) <strong>were breaking</strong> into people's houses while they (10) <strong>were sitting</strong> in their back gardens.<p>She (11) <strong>realised</strong> that she (12) <strong>didn't want</strong> to live alone any more. She (13) <strong>isn't used</strong> to being in the town yet, but it's not as difficult as she (14) <strong>thought</strong> it might be, and she loves seeing us more often.</p></div><h3>Por qué se elige cada verbo</h3><ol class=\"answers\"><li><strong>refused</strong><p>Una negativa o situación pasada ya acabada; Past Simple.</p></li><li><strong>changed</strong><p>«Last month» y «suddenly» indican un cambio puntual.</p></li><li><strong>asked</strong><p>Preguntó una vez, no era un hábito repetido.</p></li><li><strong>would lock</strong><p>Con «for years» expresa la costumbre de cerrar con llave; con «nobody», la ausencia de esa costumbre.</p></li><li><strong>used to feel</strong><p>Feel describe un estado habitual anterior; «would» no suele expresar este tipo de estado.</p></li><li><strong>met</strong><p>Se encontró con una persona en un momento concreto.</p></li><li><strong>was walking</strong><p>Estaba caminando cuando ocurrió el encuentro.</p></li><li><strong>heard</strong><p>Oyó una noticia: hecho puntual.</p></li><li><strong>were breaking</strong><p>Los robos estaban ocurriendo durante ese periodo.</p></li><li><strong>were sitting</strong><p>Acción simultánea en curso: estaban sentados en el jardín.</p></li><li><strong>realised</strong><p>«Realise» se utiliza normalmente como verbo de estado o percepción, aquí cambio puntual.</p></li><li><strong>didn't want</strong><p>«Want» es normalmente estativo; Past Simple negativo.</p></li><li><strong>isn't used</strong><p>Be used to + -ing significa estar acostumbrado, y «yet» indica que todavía no lo está.</p></li><li><strong>thought</strong><p>Describe lo que había pensado, no un proceso en desarrollo.</p></li></ol><h3>4. Be / Get used to: soluciones</h3><ol class=\"answers\"><li><strong>isn't used to</strong><p>No está acostumbrada a acostarse tarde; be used to + -ing.</p></li><li><strong>are used to</strong><p>Los niños ya están acostumbrados a ir en autobús.</p></li><li><strong>is used to</strong><p>Es su costumbre actual: dar órdenes.</p></li><li><strong>wasn't used to</strong><p>En aquel momento no estaba acostumbrada al café fuerte.</p></li><li><strong>Are you getting used to</strong><p>Pregunta por el proceso de adaptación que sigue en curso.</p></li><li><strong>got used to</strong><p>Se acostumbró al hotel en el pasado.</p></li></ol><h3>5. Past Simple / Past Continuous: soluciones</h3><ol class=\"answers\"><li><strong>was working / was playing</strong><p>Ambas acciones estaban ocurriendo a la vez.</p></li><li><strong>was looking / stopped</strong><p>Acción de fondo interrumpida por un hecho puntual.</p></li><li><strong>went / was having</strong><p>La luz se apagó durante la fiesta.</p></li><li><strong>went / stood</strong><p>Dos acciones sucesivas y terminadas.</p></li><li><strong>was hoping / said</strong><p>Una expectativa en curso frente a un resultado puntual; «hoped» también puede ser posible.</p></li></ol><h3>6. Used to: frases corregidas</h3><ol class=\"answers\"><li><strong>When I was a child, I used to go camping.</strong><p>Used to + infinitivo: costumbre del pasado.</p></li><li><strong>My parents often used to take me to the zoo.</strong><p>«Often used to» es el orden más habitual; «used to often» también se oye, aunque el libro pide corregirlo.</p></li><li><strong>They didn't use to help their parents with housework, but now they do.</strong><p>Después de «didn't» se emplea «use to», sin -d.</p></li><li><strong>We went to the beach every day last summer.</strong><p>«Last summer» delimita un periodo concreto terminado; aquí el Past Simple es la opción natural.</p></li><li><strong>Helen is a teacher at a primary school, so she teaches children.</strong><p>Enseña actualmente: Present Simple, no «used to».</p></li><li><strong>I would like to be a sports instructor because I used to train tennis players before I came to England.</strong><p>«Used to train» indica hábito pasado; «was used to training» indicaría estar acostumbrado.</p></li></ol><div class=\"links\"><strong>Para repasar:</strong><ul><li><a href=\"todos-los-tiempos-de-pasado-en-ingles.html\">Todos los tiempos de pasado en inglés</a></li><li><a href=\"used-to-would-be-used-to-get-used-to.html\">Used to, would, be used to y get used to</a></li><li><a href=\"past-tenses-ejercicios-resueltos-unidad-2.html\">Página anterior: ejercicios 1 y 2</a></li></ul></div></section>"},{"category":"grammar","title":"Linking Words of Contrast","url":"post/linking-words-of-contrast.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<section class=\"hero\"><span class=\"tag\">GRAMMAR · B1–B2</span><h1>Linking Words of Contrast: conectores de contraste</h1><p class=\"lead\">Aprende a contrastar opiniones e ideas con however, although, whereas, despite y más conectores, con ejemplos y ejercicios.</p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time></section><p>Los <strong>linking words of contrast</strong> conectan ideas opuestas y ayudan a argumentar en <em>Writing</em> y <em>Speaking</em> B1–B2.</p><h2>1. Conectores principales</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Conector</th><th>Traducción</th><th>Uso</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>But</td><td>Pero</td><td>Contraste sencillo</td></tr><tr><td>However</td><td>Sin embargo</td><td>Introduce una idea contraria</td></tr><tr><td>Although</td><td>Aunque</td><td>Seguido de sujeto y verbo</td></tr><tr><td>Even though</td><td>Aunque / A pesar de que</td><td>Contraste enfático</td></tr><tr><td>Despite</td><td>A pesar de</td><td>Sustantivo o verbo -ing</td></tr><tr><td>In spite of</td><td>A pesar de</td><td>Sustantivo o verbo -ing</td></tr><tr><td>Nevertheless / Nonetheless</td><td>No obstante</td><td>Más formal</td></tr><tr><td>Whereas / While</td><td>Mientras que</td><td>Compara dos situaciones</td></tr><tr><td>On the other hand</td><td>Por otro lado</td><td>Otra perspectiva</td></tr><tr><td>In contrast</td><td>En contraste</td><td>Marca diferencias</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>2. However y although</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>I studied all night. However, I failed the exam.</strong><em>Estudié toda la noche. Sin embargo, suspendí el examen.</em></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>Although I studied all night, I failed the exam.</strong><em>Aunque estudié toda la noche, suspendí el examen.</em></div><p><em>Although</em> introduce una oración; <em>however</em> funciona como conector entre ideas y suele ir seguido de coma al principio de una oración.</p><h2>3. Whereas, while, nevertheless y nonetheless</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>My brother loves football, whereas I prefer basketball.</strong><em>A mi hermano le encanta el fútbol, mientras que yo prefiero el baloncesto.</em></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>The task was difficult. Nevertheless, we finished it.</strong><em>La tarea era difícil. No obstante, la terminamos.</em></div><h2>4. Ejemplo de Writing B2</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>On the one hand, studying abroad can be expensive. However, it offers valuable opportunities. Although students may initially feel lonely, they often become more independent.</strong><em>Por un lado, estudiar en el extranjero puede ser caro. Sin embargo, ofrece oportunidades valiosas. Aunque al principio los estudiantes puedan sentirse solos, a menudo se vuelven más independientes.</em></div><h2>5. Practica</h2><ol><li>_____ being tired, she continued studying.</li><li>I love summer, _____ my brother prefers winter.</li><li>_____ it was raining, we went outside.</li><li>The hotel was expensive. _____, we stayed there.</li><li>He had little experience. _____, he got the job.</li></ol><details><summary>Respuestas explicadas</summary><ol><li>Despite — seguido de -ing.</li><li>Whereas — compara preferencias.</li><li>Although — seguido de oración completa.</li><li>However — introduce contraste.</li><li>Nevertheless — resultado inesperado.</li></ol><p>Algunas opciones son intercambiables si se ajusta la puntuación.</p></details><div class=\"note\"><strong>Sigue aprendiendo:</strong> <a href=\"linking-words-of-contrast.html\">Conectores de contraste</a> · <a href=\"despite-vs-in-spite-of.html\">Despite / In spite of</a> · <a href=\"police-and-crime-vocabulary.html\">Police Vocabulary</a> · <a href=\"past-tenses-ejercicios-resueltos-unidad-2.html\">Past Tenses: ejercicios resueltos</a></div>"},{"category":"grammar","title":"Despite vs. In Spite Of","url":"post/despite-vs-in-spite-of.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<section class=\"hero\"><span class=\"tag\">GRAMMAR · B1–B2</span><h1>Despite vs. In Spite Of: diferencias y ejercicios</h1><p class=\"lead\">Cuándo usar despite, in spite of, although y por qué despite of es incorrecto, con transformaciones de Cambridge B2.</p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time></section><p><strong>Despite</strong> e <strong>in spite of</strong> significan «a pesar de». <strong>Despite of</strong> es incorrecto. La diferencia principal es de estructura, no de significado.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Correcto</th><th>Incorrecto</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Despite the rain</td><td>Despite of the rain</td></tr><tr><td>In spite of the rain</td><td>Inspite of the rain</td></tr><tr><td>Despite being tired</td><td>Despite of being tired</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>1. Seguidos de sustantivo</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>Despite the rain, we went for a walk.</strong><em>A pesar de la lluvia, salimos a pasear.</em></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>In spite of her problems, she remained optimistic.</strong><em>A pesar de sus problemas, siguió siendo optimista.</em></div><h2>2. Seguidos de -ing</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>Despite feeling tired, I went to work.</strong><em>A pesar de estar cansado, fui a trabajar.</em></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>In spite of studying hard, he failed the exam.</strong><em>A pesar de estudiar mucho, suspendió el examen.</em></div><h2>3. Oración completa: the fact that</h2><p>Utiliza <strong>despite the fact that</strong> o <strong>in spite of the fact that</strong> antes de sujeto + verbo.</p><div class=\"example\"><strong>Despite the fact that it was raining, we went outside.</strong><em>A pesar de que llovía, salimos.</em></div><h2>4. Although vs. despite</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Although + oración</th><th>Despite / in spite of + nombre o -ing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Although he was tired</td><td>Despite being tired</td></tr><tr><td>Although it was raining</td><td>Despite the rain</td></tr><tr><td>Although she was nervous</td><td>In spite of being nervous</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>5. Errores frecuentes</h2><p>❌ Despite of the rain → ✅ Despite the rain<br>❌ Despite she was ill → ✅ Despite being ill / Despite the fact that she was ill<br>❌ In spite of he was tired → ✅ In spite of being tired.</p><h2>6. Transformaciones B2</h2><ol><li>Although it was cold, we went swimming. (DESPITE)</li><li>Although she was nervous, she gave a speech. (IN SPITE OF)</li><li>Despite the rain, they played tennis. (ALTHOUGH)</li><li>He passed the exam although he hadn't studied much. (DESPITE)</li></ol><details><summary>Mostrar soluciones</summary><ol><li>Despite the cold, we went swimming.</li><li>In spite of being nervous, she gave a speech.</li><li>Although it was raining, they played tennis.</li><li>Despite not having studied much, he passed the exam.</li></ol></details><p>Fuentes de consulta: <a href=\"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/despite-and-in-spite-of\">Cambridge Dictionary</a> y <a href=\"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/contrasting-ideas-although-despite-others\">British Council</a>.</p><div class=\"note\"><strong>Sigue aprendiendo:</strong> <a href=\"linking-words-of-contrast.html\">Conectores de contraste</a> · <a href=\"despite-vs-in-spite-of.html\">Despite / In spite of</a> · <a href=\"police-and-crime-vocabulary.html\">Police Vocabulary</a> · <a href=\"past-tenses-ejercicios-resueltos-unidad-2.html\">Past Tenses: ejercicios resueltos</a></div>"},{"category":"vocabulary","title":"Police and Crime Vocabulary","url":"post/police-and-crime-vocabulary.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<section class=\"hero\"><span class=\"tag\">VOCABULARY · B1–B2</span><h1>Police and Crime Vocabulary: vocabulario policial</h1><p class=\"lead\">Policía, delitos, investigación, steal vs rob, verbos irregulares y ejercicios de inglés B1–B2.</p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time></section><p>Vocabulario relacionado con policías, investigaciones y delitos, útil para leer noticias, comprender series y practicar Cambridge B2.</p><h2>1. Policía e investigación</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Police officer</td><td>Agente de policía</td></tr><tr><td>Police station</td><td>Comisaría</td></tr><tr><td>Detective</td><td>Detective</td></tr><tr><td>Investigation</td><td>Investigación</td></tr><tr><td>Suspect</td><td>Sospechoso</td></tr><tr><td>Witness</td><td>Testigo</td></tr><tr><td>Victim</td><td>Víctima</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Pruebas / indicios</td></tr><tr><td>Crime scene</td><td>Escena del crimen</td></tr><tr><td>Fingerprints</td><td>Huellas dactilares</td></tr><tr><td>Handcuffs</td><td>Esposas</td></tr><tr><td>Patrol</td><td>Patrulla / patrullar</td></tr><tr><td>Police report</td><td>Informe policial</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>2. Delitos</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Theft</td><td>Robo / hurto</td></tr><tr><td>Robbery</td><td>Robo con violencia o intimidación</td></tr><tr><td>Burglary</td><td>Entrada ilegal para cometer un delito</td></tr><tr><td>Shoplifting</td><td>Hurto en una tienda</td></tr><tr><td>Murder</td><td>Asesinato</td></tr><tr><td>Assault</td><td>Agresión</td></tr><tr><td>Kidnapping</td><td>Secuestro</td></tr><tr><td>Fraud</td><td>Estafa</td></tr><tr><td>Vandalism</td><td>Vandalismo</td></tr><tr><td>Arson</td><td>Incendio provocado</td></tr><tr><td>Pickpocketing</td><td>Hurto al descuido</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>3. Verbos y expresiones</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arrest</td><td>Detener</td></tr><tr><td>Investigate</td><td>Investigar</td></tr><tr><td>Question a witness</td><td>Interrogar a un testigo</td></tr><tr><td>Charge someone</td><td>Presentar cargos contra alguien</td></tr><tr><td>Steal</td><td>Robar algo</td></tr><tr><td>Rob</td><td>Robar a alguien / atracar un lugar</td></tr><tr><td>Break into</td><td>Entrar ilegalmente</td></tr><tr><td>Escape</td><td>Escapar</td></tr><tr><td>Catch</td><td>Atrapar</td></tr><tr><td>Release</td><td>Poner en libertad</td></tr><tr><td>Report a crime</td><td>Denunciar un delito</td></tr><tr><td>Commit a crime</td><td>Cometer un delito</td></tr><tr><td>Solve a case</td><td>Resolver un caso</td></tr><tr><td>Be found guilty</td><td>Ser declarado culpable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>4. Steal vs. rob</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>Someone stole my phone.</strong><em>Alguien me robó el teléfono. (steal + objeto)</em></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>Two men robbed a bank.</strong><em>Dos hombres atracaron un banco. (rob + lugar/persona)</em></div><h2>5. Pasado de verbos útiles</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Infinitivo</th><th>Pasado</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Steal</td><td>Stole</td><td>Robar</td></tr><tr><td>Catch</td><td>Caught</td><td>Atrapar</td></tr><tr><td>Find</td><td>Found</td><td>Encontrar</td></tr><tr><td>Break</td><td>Broke</td><td>Romper</td></tr><tr><td>Run</td><td>Ran</td><td>Correr</td></tr><tr><td>Hide</td><td>Hid</td><td>Esconder</td></tr><tr><td>Take</td><td>Took</td><td>Llevarse</td></tr><tr><td>See</td><td>Saw</td><td>Ver</td></tr><tr><td>Give</td><td>Gave</td><td>Dar</td></tr><tr><td>Leave</td><td>Left</td><td>Marcharse</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>6. Reading: A Mysterious Robbery</h2><p><strong>Last night, a jewellery shop was robbed. The police arrived shortly after the alarm went off. Two witnesses reported seeing a suspicious vehicle near the shop. Detectives collected evidence and found fingerprints at the crime scene. Although the suspect tried to escape, the police arrested him. Despite the lack of clear video footage, investigators solved the case.</strong></p><p>Anoche robaron en una joyería. La policía llegó poco después de que saltara la alarma. Dos testigos declararon haber visto un vehículo sospechoso cerca de la tienda. Los detectives recogieron pruebas y encontraron huellas en la escena del crimen. Aunque el sospechoso intentó escapar, lo detuvieron. A pesar de la falta de imágenes claras, resolvieron el caso.</p><h2>7. Ejercicios</h2><ol><li>The police _____ the suspect yesterday. (arrest)</li><li>Someone _____ my wallet last night. (steal)</li><li>The detective collected important _____.</li><li>A person who sees a crime is a _____.</li><li>The burglars _____ into the house. (break)</li></ol><details><summary>Ver respuestas</summary><ol><li>arrested</li><li>stole</li><li>evidence</li><li>witness</li><li>broke</li></ol></details><h2>8. Speaking B2</h2><div class=\"example\"><strong>In my opinion, cities should invest more in education and prevention. Furthermore, the police should work closely with local communities. Although security measures are important, preventing crime is often more effective than increasing punishment.</strong><em>En mi opinión, las ciudades deberían invertir más en educación y prevención. Además, la policía debería colaborar estrechamente con las comunidades. Aunque las medidas de seguridad son importantes, prevenir la delincuencia suele ser más eficaz que aumentar los castigos.</em></div><div class=\"note\"><strong>Sigue aprendiendo:</strong> <a href=\"linking-words-of-contrast.html\">Conectores de contraste</a> · <a href=\"despite-vs-in-spite-of.html\">Despite / In spite of</a> · <a href=\"police-and-crime-vocabulary.html\">Police Vocabulary</a> · <a href=\"past-tenses-ejercicios-resueltos-unidad-2.html\">Past Tenses: ejercicios resueltos</a></div>"},{"category":"grammar","title":"Past Tenses: ejercicios resueltos (Unidad 2)","url":"post/past-tenses-ejercicios-resueltos-unidad-2.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<h1>Past Tenses: ejercicios en blanco</h1><p>Unidad 2 · Grammar exercises. Completa los ejercicios antes de comprobar las respuestas con tu profesor o el libro.</p><h2>1. The mystery of Kaspar Hauser</h2><p><strong>Complete the text with the past simple form of these verbs.</strong></p><div class=\"verbs\">be · begin · come · drink · eat · explain · feed · find · get · give · go · have · know · learn · meet · read · seem · speak · spread · write</div><div class=\"story\"><h3>The mystery of Kaspar Hauser</h3>The mystery of Kaspar Hauser (1) ______ in Nuremberg, Germany, about 200 years ago. One morning, the people of the town (2) ______ a young man standing alone in the square. He was holding a piece of paper in his hand. The paper (3) ______ only that he (4) ______ the son of a soldier. Kaspar (5) ______ how to say a few words and when given a paper and pencil he (6) ______ his name, but he (7) ______ completely ignorant about everyday life. At first he (8) ______ only bread and (9) ______ only water, but he gradually (10) ______ used to ordinary meals. He also (11) ______ to talk properly.\n\nThe real truth about his birth remains a mystery, but it is probable that his father kept him in one small room for the whole of his early life. He (12) ______ him on bread and (13) ______ him water to drink. Kaspar never (14) ______ out, he never (15) ______ to anyone or (16) ______ other children. In spite of his extraordinary childhood, Kaspar was not stupid. He (17) ______ books and (18) ______ discussions with teachers and philosophers. News about Kaspar (19) ______ through Europe and visitors (20) ______ from abroad to meet him.</div><h2>2. Past Simple or Past Continuous</h2><p><strong>Fill in the gaps with the past simple or past continuous form of the verbs.</strong></p><ol class=\"questions\"><li>My parents ______ (get) to know each other when they ______ (study) at university.</li><li>Doctor Fisher ______ (travel) widely as a young man and ______ (always keep) a diary.</li><li>I ______ (see) my brother and his friend when I ______ (wait) for the bus, but they ______ (not see) me.</li><li>Lily ______ (fill) in the application form and ______ (give) it to the receptionist.</li><li>While I ______ (work) in Rome, I ______ (meet) a girl who ______ (look) just like your sister.</li><li>Simon ______ (buy) a new laptop because his old one ______ (always crash).</li><li>I ______ (miss) your text because my phone ______ (charge) in another room.</li><li>Anna's feeling depressed because she ______ (hope) for a pay rise last week, but she ______ (not get) one.</li></ol><section id=\"soluciones\"><h2>Respuestas y explicaciones: ejercicio 1</h2><p>Primero intenta resolver los huecos del texto de arriba. Aquí tienes la solución corregida: <strong>cada verbo de la lista se utiliza exactamente una vez</strong>. El número 3 es <strong>explained</strong> y el 17 es <strong>read</strong>.</p><div class=\"story\">The mystery of Kaspar Hauser <strong>began</strong> in Nuremberg, Germany, about 200 years ago. One morning, the people of the town <strong>found</strong> a young man standing alone in the square. He was holding a piece of paper in his hand. The paper <strong>explained</strong> only that he <strong>was</strong> the son of a soldier. Kaspar <strong>knew</strong> how to say a few words and when given a paper and pencil he <strong>wrote</strong> his name, but he <strong>seemed</strong> completely ignorant about everyday life. At first he <strong>ate</strong> only bread and <strong>drank</strong> only water, but he gradually <strong>got</strong> used to ordinary meals. He also <strong>learnt</strong> to talk properly.<p>The real truth about his birth remains a mystery, but it is probable that his father kept him in one small room for the whole of his early life. He <strong>fed</strong> him on bread and <strong>gave</strong> him water to drink. Kaspar never <strong>went</strong> out, he never <strong>spoke</strong> to anyone or <strong>met</strong> other children. In spite of his extraordinary childhood, Kaspar was not stupid. He <strong>read</strong> books and <strong>had</strong> discussions with teachers and philosophers. News about Kaspar <strong>spread</strong> through Europe and visitors <strong>came</strong> from abroad to meet him.</p></div><h3>Explicación de las 20 respuestas</h3><table><thead><tr><th>N.º</th><th>Infinitivo</th><th>Respuesta</th><th>Por qué</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>begin</td><td><strong>began</strong></td><td>comenzó; hecho del pasado</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>find</td><td><strong>found</strong></td><td>encontraron; descubrimiento puntual</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>explain</td><td><strong>explained</strong></td><td>explicaba el contenido del papel</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>be</td><td><strong>was</strong></td><td>he es singular</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>know</td><td><strong>knew</strong></td><td>sabía; verbo de estado</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>write</td><td><strong>wrote</strong></td><td>escribió su nombre</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>seem</td><td><strong>seemed</strong></td><td>parecía; verbo regular</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>eat</td><td><strong>ate</strong></td><td>comía pan en aquella etapa</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>drink</td><td><strong>drank</strong></td><td>bebía agua</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>get</td><td><strong>got</strong></td><td>got used to: se acostumbró</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>learn</td><td><strong>learnt / learned</strong></td><td>aprendió; ambas formas válidas</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>feed</td><td><strong>fed</strong></td><td>alimentaba con pan</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>give</td><td><strong>gave</strong></td><td>le daba agua</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>go</td><td><strong>went</strong></td><td>nunca salía</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>speak</td><td><strong>spoke</strong></td><td>nunca hablaba</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>meet</td><td><strong>met</strong></td><td>nunca conocía a otros niños</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>read</td><td><strong>read</strong></td><td>leía; en pasado suena /red/</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>have</td><td><strong>had</strong></td><td>mantenía conversaciones</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>spread</td><td><strong>spread</strong></td><td>las noticias se difundieron</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>come</td><td><strong>came</strong></td><td>vinieron visitantes</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Recuerda:</strong> en narraciones históricas usamos Past Simple para acontecimientos y estados pasados. Aunque en español algunos verbos se traduzcan en imperfecto, el inglés puede emplear el mismo Past Simple.</p><h3>Artículos para repasar</h3><ul><li><a href=\"50-verbos-irregulares-en-ingles.html\">50 verbos irregulares en inglés</a></li><li><a href=\"todos-los-tiempos-de-pasado-en-ingles.html\">Todos los tiempos de pasado</a></li><li><a href=\"used-to-would-be-used-to-get-used-to.html\">Used to y get used to</a></li></ul></section>"},{"category":"grammar","title":"Used to, would, be used to y get used to","url":"post/used-to-would-be-used-to-get-used-to.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<section class=\"hero\"><span class=\"tag\">GRAMMAR · B1–B2</span><h1>Used to, would, be used to y get used to: diferencias y ejercicios</h1><p class=\"lead\">Cuatro estructuras parecidas que expresan cosas distintas: hábitos pasados, estados anteriores y adaptación.</p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time></section>\n<h2>1. Used to + infinitivo</h2><p>Describe <strong>hábitos o estados habituales del pasado</strong> que ya no son ciertos o que contrastan con el presente.</p><div class=\"example\"><strong>I used to walk to school.</strong><span>Antes iba andando al colegio (ahora ya no).</span></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>She used to live near the sea.</strong><span>Antes vivía cerca del mar.</span></div><p><strong>Negativa:</strong> I <strong>didn't use to</strong> drink coffee. (Antes no bebía café). <strong>Pregunta:</strong> <strong>Did</strong> you <strong>use to</strong> play tennis? (¿Solías jugar al tenis?). Después de <em>did</em> escribimos <strong>use</strong>, sin -d.</p>\n<h2>2. Would + infinitivo</h2><p>Se usa para <strong>acciones repetidas</strong> en el pasado, especialmente al contar recuerdos. No se emplea normalmente para describir estados pasados como <em>know, be, have</em> (posesión) o <em>live</em>.</p><div class=\"example\"><strong>Every summer, we would visit our grandparents.</strong><span>Todos los veranos visitábamos a nuestros abuelos.</span></div><p>Para el estado «Antes era tímido», escribe <strong>I used to be shy</strong>, no «I would be shy» con ese significado.</p><p><strong>Ojo:</strong> <em>would</em> también expresa condicionales y otras ideas; aquí hablamos solo de su uso para hábitos pasados.</p>\n<h2>3. Be used to + sustantivo / verbo en -ing</h2><p>Significa <strong>estar acostumbrado a</strong>. El verbo <em>be</em> puede cambiar de tiempo (am/is/are/was/were).</p><div class=\"example\"><strong>She is used to getting up early.</strong><span>Está acostumbrada a levantarse temprano.</span></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>They weren't used to the cold.</strong><span>No estaban acostumbrados al frío.</span></div><p>La palabra <em>to</em> aquí es una preposición: por eso escribimos <strong>to getting</strong>, no «to get».</p>\n<h2>4. Get used to + sustantivo / verbo en -ing</h2><p>Indica <strong>acostumbrarse</strong>: el proceso de adaptación, no simplemente el estado de estar acostumbrado.</p><div class=\"example\"><strong>I'm getting used to my new job.</strong><span>Me estoy acostumbrando a mi nuevo trabajo.</span></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>At first the hotel felt strange, but I soon got used to it.</strong><span>Al principio el hotel me resultaba extraño, pero pronto me acostumbré.</span></div>\n<h2>5. Comparación rápida</h2><div class=\"scroll\"><table><tr><th>Estructura</th><th>Significado</th><th>Ejemplo</th></tr><tr><td>used to + verbo base</td><td>Antes solía…</td><td>I used to cycle.</td></tr><tr><td>would + verbo base</td><td>Acción habitual pasada</td><td>We would cycle every Sunday.</td></tr><tr><td>be used to + -ing / nombre</td><td>Estar acostumbrado</td><td>I'm used to cycling.</td></tr><tr><td>get used to + -ing / nombre</td><td>Acostumbrarse</td><td>I'm getting used to cycling.</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>6. Errores frecuentes</h2><div class=\"box\"><p>❌ I am used to wake up early.<br>✅ <strong>I am used to waking up early.</strong></p><p>❌ Did you used to go there?<br>✅ <strong>Did you use to go there?</strong></p><p>❌ I would have a small house when I was young. (describiendo posesión pasada)<br>✅ <strong>I used to have a small house.</strong></p><p>❌ She didn't used to like tea.<br>✅ <strong>She didn't use to like tea.</strong></p></div>\n<h2>7. Ejercicios originales B1–B2</h2><p>Completa con la forma correcta de <em>used to, would, be used to</em> o <em>get used to</em>. Fíjate en el significado y en el tiempo verbal.</p><div class=\"box\"><ol><li>When I was little, I ______ (play) outside every evening.</li><li>My new colleague is from Finland, so she ______ (work) in cold weather.</li><li>At first, I couldn't sleep in the city, but I gradually ______ (live) with the noise.</li><li>My grandparents ______ (tell) us stories every winter.</li><li>He ______ (not / like) vegetables, but now he loves them.</li><li>Are you ______ (drive) on the left yet?</li><li>Did your sister ______ (live) in Manchester?</li><li>After a month, they finally ______ (wake up) early.</li></ol></div>\n<details><summary>Ver soluciones y explicaciones</summary><ol><li><strong>used to play</strong> (también <em>would play</em> si se cuenta como hábito repetido).</li><li><strong>is used to working</strong> — ya está acostumbrada.</li><li><strong>got used to living</strong> — proceso de adaptación terminado.</li><li><strong>would tell</strong> (también <em>used to tell</em>) — repetición habitual.</li><li><strong>didn't use to like</strong> — estado pasado en contraste con ahora.</li><li><strong>used to driving</strong> — detrás de <em>are you</em>, pregunta por estar acostumbrado.</li><li><strong>use to live</strong> — tras <em>did</em>, sin -d.</li><li><strong>got used to waking up</strong> — llegaron a acostumbrarse.</li></ol></details>\n<h2>8. Práctica oral</h2><p>Habla durante un minuto: ¿qué cosas hacías de niño, cuáles ya no haces y a qué te has tenido que acostumbrar últimamente? Usa al menos un ejemplo con <em>used to</em>, <em>would</em> y <em>get used to</em>.</p><div class=\"box\"><strong>Continúa repasando:</strong> <a href=\"todos-los-tiempos-de-pasado-en-ingles.html\">Todos los tiempos de pasado en inglés</a> · <a href=\"../index.html\">Más artículos</a></div>"},{"category":"speaking","title":"Cómo expresar opiniones en inglés y no quedarte en blanco","url":"post/expresiones-opiniones-no-quedarte-en-blanco.html","date":"2026-10-09","html":"<div class=\"hero\"><span class=\"tag\">Speaking · B1–B2</span><h1>Cómo expresar opiniones en inglés y no quedarte en blanco</h1><p class=\"lead\">Conectores, frases para debatir y expresiones para ganar tiempo al hablar, con ejemplos y traducción al español.</p><p><time datetime=\"2026-10-09\">9 de octubre de 2026</time> · jnfz92</p></div>\n<p>¿Alguna vez has querido dar tu opinión en inglés y no has sabido cómo continuar? No necesitas palabras complicadas: conocer unas cuantas expresiones te ayudará a ordenar tus ideas y mantener la conversación.</p>\n<div class=\"toc\"><strong>En este artículo</strong><ol><li><a href=\"#s1\">Para dar tu opinión</a></li><li><a href=\"#s2\">Para contrastar ideas</a></li><li><a href=\"#s3\">Para mostrar acuerdo</a></li><li><a href=\"#s4\">Para discrepar educadamente</a></li><li><a href=\"#s5\">Cuando te quedas en blanco</a></li><li><a href=\"#s6\">Cuando olvidas una palabra</a></li><li><a href=\"#s7\">Para añadir argumentos</a></li><li><a href=\"#s8\">Para concluir</a></li><li><a href=\"#modelo\">Modelo completo</a></li><li><a href=\"#consejos\">Consejos y errores frecuentes</a></li></ol></div>\n<section id=\"s1\"><h2>1. Para dar tu opinión</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I think that…</strong></td><td>Creo que…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In my opinion…</strong></td><td>En mi opinión…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>From my point of view…</strong></td><td>Desde mi punto de vista…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personally, I think…</strong></td><td>Personalmente, creo que…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The way I see it…</strong></td><td>Tal como yo lo veo…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>As far as I'm concerned…</strong></td><td>Por lo que a mí respecta…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>In my opinion, learning English opens many doors.</strong><span>En mi opinión, aprender inglés abre muchas puertas.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s2\"><h2>2. Para contrastar ideas</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>However</strong></td><td>Sin embargo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>On the other hand</strong></td><td>Por otro lado</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nevertheless</strong></td><td>No obstante</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Although / Even though</strong></td><td>Aunque</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Whereas / While</strong></td><td>Mientras que / En cambio</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In contrast</strong></td><td>En contraste</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Despite</strong></td><td>A pesar de</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>I like living in the city. However, it can be stressful.</strong><span>Me gusta vivir en la ciudad. Sin embargo, puede ser estresante.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s3\"><h2>3. Para mostrar acuerdo</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I agree</strong></td><td>Estoy de acuerdo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Absolutely!</strong></td><td>¡Totalmente!</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exactly!</strong></td><td>¡Exactamente!</td></tr><tr><td><strong>That's a good point</strong></td><td>Es un buen argumento</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I couldn't agree more</strong></td><td>No podría estar más de acuerdo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I feel the same way</strong></td><td>Pienso lo mismo</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>I couldn't agree more. Education is very important.</strong><span>No podría estar más de acuerdo. La educación es muy importante.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s4\"><h2>4. Para discrepar educadamente</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I disagree</strong></td><td>No estoy de acuerdo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I'm not sure about that</strong></td><td>No estoy seguro de eso</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I see your point, but…</strong></td><td>Entiendo tu postura, pero…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>That's not necessarily true</strong></td><td>Eso no es necesariamente cierto</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I see what you mean; however…</strong></td><td>Entiendo lo que dices; sin embargo…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>I see your point, but I think studying abroad has more advantages.</strong><span>Entiendo tu postura, pero creo que estudiar en el extranjero tiene más ventajas.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s5\"><h2>5. Cuando te quedas en blanco</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Well…</strong></td><td>Bueno…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Let me think…</strong></td><td>Déjame pensar…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>That's a good question</strong></td><td>Buena pregunta</td></tr><tr><td><strong>How can I put it?</strong></td><td>¿Cómo podría decirlo?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>What I mean is…</strong></td><td>Lo que quiero decir es…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Let me see…</strong></td><td>A ver…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I've lost my train of thought</strong></td><td>He perdido el hilo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The word is on the tip of my tongue</strong></td><td>Tengo la palabra en la punta de la lengua</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Where was I?</strong></td><td>¿Por dónde iba?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>Well, that's a good question. Let me think for a second.</strong><span>Bueno, es una buena pregunta. Déjame pensar un segundo.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s6\"><h2>6. Cuando olvidas una palabra</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>How do you say…?</strong></td><td>¿Cómo se dice…?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>What's the word for…?</strong></td><td>¿Cuál es la palabra para…?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>It's something you use to…</strong></td><td>Es algo que utilizas para…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>It's a kind of…</strong></td><td>Es una especie de…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>I can't remember the word</strong></td><td>No recuerdo la palabra</td></tr><tr><td><strong>What I'm trying to say is…</strong></td><td>Lo que intento decir es…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>I can't remember the word, but it's something you use to open a bottle.</strong><span>No recuerdo la palabra, pero es algo que utilizas para abrir una botella.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s7\"><h2>7. Para añadir argumentos</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Furthermore</strong></td><td>Además</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Moreover</strong></td><td>Es más / Además</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In addition</strong></td><td>Además</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Besides</strong></td><td>Aparte de eso</td></tr><tr><td><strong>What's more</strong></td><td>Es más</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Another reason is…</strong></td><td>Otra razón es…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>Learning English is useful. Moreover, it can improve your job opportunities.</strong><span>Aprender inglés es útil. Además, puede mejorar tus oportunidades laborales.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"s8\"><h2>8. Para concluir</h2><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inglés</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>In conclusion</strong></td><td>En conclusión</td></tr><tr><td><strong>To sum up</strong></td><td>En resumen</td></tr><tr><td><strong>All in all</strong></td><td>En definitiva</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Overall</strong></td><td>En general</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In short</strong></td><td>En pocas palabras</td></tr><tr><td><strong>That's why I think…</strong></td><td>Por eso creo que…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"example\"><strong>To sum up, both American and British English are equally useful.</strong><span>En resumen, tanto el inglés americano como el británico son igual de útiles.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"modelo\"><h2>9. Modelo completo de speaking</h2><p><strong>Pregunta:</strong> Is it better to study online or in a classroom? (¿Es mejor estudiar online o en clase?)</p>\n<div class=\"example\"><strong>Well, that's an interesting question. Personally, I think studying online has many advantages.</strong><span>Bueno, es una pregunta interesante. Personalmente, creo que estudiar online tiene muchas ventajas.</span></div>\n<div class=\"example\"><strong>On the one hand, it is more flexible and convenient. On the other hand, studying in a classroom allows students to interact with their teachers.</strong><span>Por un lado, es más flexible y cómodo. Por otro, estudiar en clase permite a los alumnos interactuar con sus profesores.</span></div>\n<div class=\"example\"><strong>However, I believe that online learning is a great option for people who work. To sum up, both methods have advantages, but I prefer studying online.</strong><span>Sin embargo, creo que el aprendizaje online es una gran opción para quienes trabajan. En resumen, ambos métodos tienen ventajas, pero prefiero estudiar online.</span></div></section>\n<section id=\"consejos\"><h2>10. Consejos y errores frecuentes</h2><div class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Empieza por cinco expresiones:</strong> Well… · Let me think… · In my opinion… · However… · What I mean is…</p><p><strong>Ojo con la gramática:</strong> <em>Although</em> introduce una oración («Although it was raining…»), mientras que <em>despite</em> suele ir seguido de sustantivo o verbo en -ing («Despite the rain…» / «Despite raining…»). <em>On the other hand</em> contrasta dos aspectos; <em>however</em> también puede introducir una objeción. En una conversación informal no abuses de conectores muy formales como <em>moreover</em> o <em>nevertheless</em>.</p><p>Hacer pausas no significa hablar mal. Practica enlazando una opinión, una razón, un contraste y una conclusión.</p></div></section>\n<div class=\"practice\"><h2>Mini ejercicio</h2><p>Responde en voz alta: <strong>Should students use smartphones at school?</strong></p><p>Intenta incluir «In my opinion», «On the other hand», «Let me think» y «To sum up».</p></div>\n<p><strong>Etiquetas:</strong> Speaking, English Vocabulary, Conectores, B1, B2, jnfz92</p>"},{"category":"grammar","title":"50 verbos irregulares en inglés","url":"post/50-verbos-irregulares-en-ingles.html","date":"2026-10-08","html":"<section class=\"hero\"><span class=\"tag\">Grammar · Vocabulary</span><h1>50 verbos irregulares en inglés: lista completa para estudiar</h1><p class=\"lead\">Infinitivo, pasado simple, participio y traducción al español; aprende a usarlos con ejemplos y ponlos a prueba.</p><p><strong>Nivel:</strong> A1–A2 (útil también para repasar B1) · <time datetime=\"2026-10-08\">8 de octubre de 2026</time></p></section>\n<article class=\"article\">\n\n<h2 id=\"que-son\">¿Qué son los verbos irregulares?</h2>\n<p>En inglés, muchos verbos forman el pasado añadiendo <strong>-ed</strong>: <em>work → worked</em> (trabajar → trabajó). Los <strong>verbos irregulares</strong> no siguen esa regla de manera general. Sus formas deben aprenderse: <em>go → went → gone</em> (ir → fue → ido).</p>\n<p>En las listas encontrarás tres formas: <strong>infinitivo</strong> (forma base, sin <em>to</em>), <strong>past simple</strong> (pasado simple) y <strong>past participle</strong> (participio pasado). La traducción indica el significado habitual, que puede variar según el contexto.</p>\n<div class=\"example\"><strong>Ejemplo:</strong> I went home yesterday. <span class=\"translation\">— Ayer fui a casa.</span><br>I have gone home. <span class=\"translation\">— Me he ido a casa.</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"tabla\">Lista de los 50 verbos irregulares más útiles</h2>\n<p>Esta selección reúne 50 verbos muy frecuentes en conversaciones y textos cotidianos. En móvil, desliza la tabla horizontalmente para ver todas las columnas.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Tabla de 50 verbos irregulares\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th>N.º</th><th>Infinitivo</th><th>Past simple</th><th>Past participle</th><th>Español</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>be</td><td>was / were</td><td>been</td><td>ser / estar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>become</td><td>became</td><td>become</td><td>convertirse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>begin</td><td>began</td><td>begun</td><td>empezar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>break</td><td>broke</td><td>broken</td><td>romper</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>bring</td><td>brought</td><td>brought</td><td>traer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>build</td><td>built</td><td>built</td><td>construir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>buy</td><td>bought</td><td>bought</td><td>comprar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>catch</td><td>caught</td><td>caught</td><td>atrapar / coger</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9</td><td>choose</td><td>chose</td><td>chosen</td><td>elegir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>come</td><td>came</td><td>come</td><td>venir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11</td><td>cost</td><td>cost</td><td>cost</td><td>costar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>cut</td><td>cut</td><td>cut</td><td>cortar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>do</td><td>did</td><td>done</td><td>hacer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14</td><td>drink</td><td>drank</td><td>drunk</td><td>beber</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>drive</td><td>drove</td><td>driven</td><td>conducir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>eat</td><td>ate</td><td>eaten</td><td>comer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>17</td><td>fall</td><td>fell</td><td>fallen</td><td>caer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>feel</td><td>felt</td><td>felt</td><td>sentir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>19</td><td>find</td><td>found</td><td>found</td><td>encontrar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>20</td><td>fly</td><td>flew</td><td>flown</td><td>volar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>21</td><td>forget</td><td>forgot</td><td>forgotten</td><td>olvidar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>22</td><td>get</td><td>got</td><td>got / gotten</td><td>obtener / conseguir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>23</td><td>give</td><td>gave</td><td>given</td><td>dar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>24</td><td>go</td><td>went</td><td>gone</td><td>ir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>25</td><td>grow</td><td>grew</td><td>grown</td><td>crecer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>26</td><td>have</td><td>had</td><td>had</td><td>tener / haber</td></tr>\n<tr><td>27</td><td>hear</td><td>heard</td><td>heard</td><td>oír</td></tr>\n<tr><td>28</td><td>know</td><td>knew</td><td>known</td><td>saber / conocer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>29</td><td>leave</td><td>left</td><td>left</td><td>salir / dejar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>30</td><td>lose</td><td>lost</td><td>lost</td><td>perder</td></tr>\n<tr><td>31</td><td>make</td><td>made</td><td>made</td><td>hacer / fabricar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>32</td><td>meet</td><td>met</td><td>met</td><td>conocer / reunirse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>33</td><td>pay</td><td>paid</td><td>paid</td><td>pagar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>34</td><td>put</td><td>put</td><td>put</td><td>poner</td></tr>\n<tr><td>35</td><td>read</td><td>read</td><td>read</td><td>leer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>36</td><td>ride</td><td>rode</td><td>ridden</td><td>montar / ir en</td></tr>\n<tr><td>37</td><td>run</td><td>ran</td><td>run</td><td>correr</td></tr>\n<tr><td>38</td><td>say</td><td>said</td><td>said</td><td>decir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>39</td><td>see</td><td>saw</td><td>seen</td><td>ver</td></tr>\n<tr><td>40</td><td>sell</td><td>sold</td><td>sold</td><td>vender</td></tr>\n<tr><td>41</td><td>send</td><td>sent</td><td>sent</td><td>enviar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>42</td><td>sing</td><td>sang</td><td>sung</td><td>cantar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>43</td><td>sit</td><td>sat</td><td>sat</td><td>sentarse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>44</td><td>sleep</td><td>slept</td><td>slept</td><td>dormir</td></tr>\n<tr><td>45</td><td>speak</td><td>spoke</td><td>spoken</td><td>hablar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>46</td><td>swim</td><td>swam</td><td>swum</td><td>nadar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>47</td><td>take</td><td>took</td><td>taken</td><td>tomar / llevar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>48</td><td>teach</td><td>taught</td><td>taught</td><td>enseñar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>49</td><td>think</td><td>thought</td><td>thought</td><td>pensar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>50</td><td>write</td><td>wrote</td><td>written</td><td>escribir</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"note\"><strong>Detalles importantes:</strong> <em>read</em> se escribe igual en las tres formas, pero el pasado y el participio se pronuncian /red/ (el infinitivo /riːd/). En <em>get</em>, <em>gotten</em> es especialmente habitual como participio en inglés estadounidense; <em>got</em> es frecuente en inglés británico. <em>Be</em> utiliza <em>was</em> y <em>were</em> según el sujeto.</div>\n<h2 id=\"usos\">¿Cuándo usar el pasado simple y el participio?</h2>\n<h3>1. Past simple: acciones terminadas</h3><p>Usa la segunda columna al contar algo que ocurrió en un momento del pasado. En afirmativa, no añadas <em>-ed</em> a estos verbos.</p>\n<div class=\"example\">She <strong>ate</strong> breakfast at eight. <span class=\"translation\">— Ella desayunó a las ocho.</span><br>We <strong>saw</strong> a film last night. <span class=\"translation\">— Vimos una película anoche.</span><br>They <strong>bought</strong> a car. <span class=\"translation\">— Compraron un coche.</span></div>\n<h3>2. Past participle: tiempos perfectos y voz pasiva</h3><p>La tercera columna aparece, por ejemplo, con <em>have/has</em> en present perfect o con <em>had</em> en past perfect. También se usa en pasiva con una forma de <em>be</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"example\">I have <strong>written</strong> an email. <span class=\"translation\">— He escrito un correo electrónico.</span><br>She has <strong>broken</strong> her phone. <span class=\"translation\">— Se le ha roto el móvil.</span><br>The house was <strong>built</strong> in 1990. <span class=\"translation\">— La casa fue construida en 1990.</span></div>\n<h3>3. Preguntas y negaciones en past simple</h3><p>Con <em>did</em> o <em>didn't</em>, el verbo principal vuelve a la <strong>forma base</strong>. El verbo <em>be</em> funciona de otra manera: <em>Was she here?</em> (¿Estaba ella aquí?).</p>\n<div class=\"example\">Did you <strong>go</strong> to school? <span class=\"translation\">— ¿Fuiste al colegio?</span><br>I didn't <strong>see</strong> him. <span class=\"translation\">— No lo vi.</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"patrones\">Trucos para memorizar los 50 verbos</h2>\n<p>Aprenderlos por patrones es más fácil que intentar memorizar una lista completa de golpe:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Las tres formas iguales:</strong> cut–cut–cut, put–put–put, cost–cost–cost.</li><li><strong>Pasado y participio iguales:</strong> buy–bought–bought, feel–felt–felt, have–had–had, teach–taught–taught.</li><li><strong>Cambios de vocal:</strong> sing–sang–sung, swim–swam–swum, begin–began–begun.</li><li><strong>Las tres formas distintas:</strong> write–wrote–written, go–went–gone, see–saw–seen.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"note\"><strong>Plan de cinco días:</strong> estudia 10 verbos diarios. Para cada uno, di las tres formas, escribe la traducción y crea una frase en pasado simple y otra en present perfect. Repasa los anteriores antes de continuar.</div>\n<h2 id=\"errores\">Errores frecuentes entre hispanohablantes</h2>\n<div class=\"warning\"><strong>Incorrecto:</strong> I have went home.<br><strong>Correcto:</strong> I have gone home. <span class=\"translation\">— Me he ido a casa.</span><p>Después de <em>have</em> necesitas el participio (<em>gone</em>), no el pasado simple (<em>went</em>).</p></div>\n<div class=\"warning\"><strong>Incorrecto:</strong> Did you saw the film?<br><strong>Correcto:</strong> Did you see the film? <span class=\"translation\">— ¿Viste la película?</span><p>Después de <em>did</em> se utiliza el infinitivo sin <em>to</em>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"warning\"><strong>Incorrecto:</strong> She buyed a book.<br><strong>Correcto:</strong> She bought a book. <span class=\"translation\">— Ella compró un libro.</span><p><em>Buy</em> es irregular: <em>bought</em>, no <em>buyed</em>.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"ejercicios\">Ejercicios para practicar</h2>\n<p>Completa las frases con la forma correcta del verbo entre paréntesis. Primero intenta resolverlas sin mirar la tabla. Las traducciones muestran el sentido de cada oración.</p>\n<ol><li>Yesterday I _____ (go) to the park. <span class=\"translation\">— Ayer fui al parque.</span></li>\n<li>She has _____ (write) a letter. <span class=\"translation\">— Ella ha escrito una carta.</span></li>\n<li>We _____ (eat) pizza last night. <span class=\"translation\">— Comimos pizza anoche.</span></li>\n<li>Have you ever _____ (fly) in a plane? <span class=\"translation\">— ¿Has volado alguna vez en avión?</span></li>\n<li>He _____ (buy) a new phone yesterday. <span class=\"translation\">— Ayer compró un móvil nuevo.</span></li>\n<li>They have _____ (see) that film. <span class=\"translation\">— Han visto esa película.</span></li>\n<li>Did you _____ (take) the bus? <span class=\"translation\">— ¿Cogiste el autobús?</span></li>\n<li>I didn't _____ (know) the answer. <span class=\"translation\">— No sabía la respuesta.</span></li>\n<li>My sister _____ (teach) English last year. <span class=\"translation\">— Mi hermana enseñó inglés el año pasado.</span></li>\n<li>We have _____ (choose) a restaurant. <span class=\"translation\">— Hemos elegido un restaurante.</span></li></ol>\n<details><summary>Ver soluciones de los 10 ejercicios</summary><ol><li>went — past simple de go</li><li>written — participio de write</li><li>ate — past simple de eat</li><li>flown — participio de fly</li><li>bought — past simple de buy</li><li>seen — participio de see</li><li>take — forma base después de did</li><li>know — forma base después de didn't</li><li>taught — past simple de teach</li><li>chosen — participio de choose</li></ol></details>\n<h3>Reto final</h3><p>Escribe cinco frases sobre lo que hiciste ayer utilizando <em>went, ate, saw, bought</em> y <em>met</em>. Después transforma dos de ellas en frases con present perfect cuando el significado lo permita. Traduce cada frase al español y comprueba que has elegido la segunda o la tercera forma correctamente.</p>\n<div class=\"next\"><strong>¿Quieres seguir practicando?</strong><p>Aprende a distinguir el pasado simple y el present perfect con más ejemplos bilingües.</p><a href=\"present-perfect-vs-past-simple.html\">Ver Present Perfect vs. Past Simple →</a><p><a href=\"../index.html\">Volver a todos los artículos →</a></p></div>\n</article>"}]);